{"birds": [{"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 1, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "8:59 AM", "ts": 1793192354, "mins": [539], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APAAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAH+AAEB/wADAf+ADgH/wBwB/8BwAf/h4AD/84AA//4AAH/8AAA/+AAAH/wAAA/+AAA/PgAAeA8AAfgHgAegB8AOAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 5, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "9:03 AM", "ts": 1793192356, "mins": [539, 541, 542, 543, 543], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/AwAD/wYAA/8EAAf/NAAH/zwAB/98AA//+AAP//4AH/+/AD//PgA//38Af/98AH//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4AHg/wADgH4ABwA+AA4APAAcAH4AHAB8ADgA9AAwAeAAIAPAAAAHwAAABwAAAA4AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 3, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "9:03 AM", "ts": 1793192388, "mins": [539, 542, 543], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "A+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD/9AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+fAAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 3, "first": "9:00 AM", "last": "9:03 AM", "ts": 1793192409, "mins": [540, 541, 543], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPAAAAD+AAAAfwAAAH+AAAD/4AAA//AAAf/gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAA//4AAf/+AAP//gAH//zAB//5wA/89wAf+OcAP38MAD9+PgAfh/gAPgHwAHgD4ADwA4AB8AMAA+AGAAPADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 1, "first": "9:01 AM", "last": "9:01 AM", "ts": 1793192515, "mins": [541], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask":